@page
@model WoodruffDev.Pages.BlogModel
@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Blog & Insights";
}

<div class="page-header">
    <div class="container">
        <span class="section-label">Blog &amp; Insights</span>
        <h1>Blog &amp; Insights</h1>
        <p>Thoughts on software architecture, distributed systems, patterns, and the craft of building lasting software.</p>
    </div>
</div>

<section class="section">
    <div class="container">
        <div class="blog-list-item fade-in">
            <div class="blog-list-img">
                <img src="/images/blog-lease-pattern.jpg" alt="Lease Pattern"
                     onerror="this.parentElement.innerHTML='<div style=\'display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;height:100%;font-size:2rem;color:#c9a96e\'>&#128221;</div>'" />
            </div>
            <div class="blog-list-body">
                <div class="blog-list-meta">
                    <span class="blog-card-tag">Patterns</span>
                    <span>February 2026</span>
                </div>
                <h3><a href="https://woodruff.dev/lease-pattern-in-net-a-lock-with-an-expiration-date-that-saves-your-data/" target="_blank">Lease Pattern in .NET: A Lock With an Expiration Date That Saves Your Data</a></h3>
                <p>Indefinite locks belong to a world where processes never crash and networks never split. That world does not exist. A lease fixes that by putting a deadline on ownership.</p>
            </div>
        </div>
        <div class="blog-list-item fade-in">
            <div class="blog-list-img">
                <img src="/images/blog-leader-election.jpg" alt="Leader Election"
                     onerror="this.parentElement.innerHTML='<div style=\'display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;height:100%;font-size:2rem;color:#c9a96e\'>&#128221;</div>'" />
            </div>
            <div class="blog-list-body">
                <div class="blog-list-meta">
                    <span class="blog-card-tag">Patterns</span>
                    <span>February 2026</span>
                </div>
                <h3><a href="https://woodruff.dev/leader-election-in-net-picking-one-boss-without-creating-two/" target="_blank">Leader Election in .NET: Picking One Boss Without Creating Two</a></h3>
                <p>Leader election is the pattern that turns &ldquo;somebody should run this&rdquo; into &ldquo;exactly one node is allowed to run this, and it must be provable.&rdquo;</p>
            </div>
        </div>
        <div class="blog-list-item fade-in">
            <div class="blog-list-img">
                <img src="/images/blog-leader-followers.jpg" alt="Leader and Followers"
                     onerror="this.parentElement.innerHTML='<div style=\'display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;height:100%;font-size:2rem;color:#c9a96e\'>&#128221;</div>'" />
            </div>
            <div class="blog-list-body">
                <div class="blog-list-meta">
                    <span class="blog-card-tag">Patterns</span>
                    <span>January 2026</span>
                </div>
                <h3><a href="https://woodruff.dev/distributed-system-pattern-leader-and-followers-in-net-one-decision-maker-many-replicas-fewer-outages/" target="_blank">Distributed System Pattern: Leader and Followers in .NET</a></h3>
                <p>Distributed systems rarely fail because you picked the wrong cloud service. They fail because two nodes believe they are in charge.</p>
            </div>
        </div>
        <div class="blog-list-item fade-in">
            <div class="blog-list-img">
                <img src="/images/blog-systems-thinking.jpg" alt="Systems Thinking"
